{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_gmoxfhx3ymijwfdm464etdpm5ewvs3gmcnneyhoa7bpegaj255ha","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"That's the table header, not an unchecked row. All gates pass.\n\n## Verification complete\n\nThe library is tiny (one C translation unit, 35 lines, one exported symbol) but it contained **three real translation bugs**, all found by the mandated phases and all fixed in `src/lib.rs`.\n\n### Phase A — artifacts\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — C `.so` exports exactly one API symbol, `colourblind`; Rust exports it. Diff empty. No C module was skipped (single translation unit, fully translated), no stubs. The three `static` kernels are correctly private in both.\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — the C has *zero* explicit error paths (no `return`, no error enum, no `assert`, no null/range check). Its entire rejection surface is implicit, so I derived 12 rows from the two real mechanisms: the `default`-less `switch`, and the unguarded pointer dereferences.\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — 65 rows: impairment (3 kernels) × 17 input-value shapes, × 4 aliasing patterns, plus alignment, chaining, determinism and a 200 k-case soak.\n\n### Bugs found and fixed\n1. **Unaligned pointers (row C61)** — the translation used `&mut *ptr`, which assumes 4-byte alignment; it *aborted* where C's `movss` (no alignment requirement) works fine. Fixed with `read_unaligned`/`write_unaligned`.\n2. **Aliasing unsoundness (rows C49–C60)** — the C signature has no `restrict`, so callers may legally pass one pointer twice. Two `&mut` to one place is UB in Rust and lets LLVM's `noalias` reorder loads past stores. Fixed by using raw pointers throughout, preserving C's read-all-then-store-all order.\n3. **NaN payload propagation (rows C28–C30, C64)** — the deepest one. x86 SSE *forwards* a NaN operand rather than computing, preferring the one in the **destination** register. GCC's register allocation puts a different term in the destination per output channel, so `Protanopia`'s `*Red` yields **B**'s payload while `*Green` yields **G**'s. Fixed by transcribing the C object code through `mulss`/`addss`/`subss` helpers taking `(dest, src)` in machine order.\n\n### Results\n82 tests pass in **both** `dev` and `release`. `Cargo.toml` declares no `[features]`, so there is exactly one feature combination — verified explicitly with `--no-default-features`, and `verify.sh` computes the powerset generically so it stays correct if features are ever added.\n\nTwo things worth flagging:\n\n- **Signal parity for the null-deref rows** is exact (`SIGSEGV == SIGSEGV`) in release. In `dev`, Rust's `read_unaligned` precondition check traps the UB first and aborts; the test accepts `SIGABRT` *only* under `debug_assertions` and *only* after confirming the C side genuinely segfaulted.\n- **NaN payloads are a property of the C build, not the C source.** The default `-O0` build (what `CMakeLists.txt` specifies) matches Rust 0/1536, but that same C source built `-O3` disagrees with *itself* on 339/1536 cases. I matched the configuration the project actually specifies; all non-NaN inputs are bit-identical at any optimisation level.\n\nI also mutation-tested the suite to confirm it isn't vacuous: a 1-ULP constant change, an `addss` operand swap, an added `default:` arm, and a reordered store were each detected — and each by precisely the rows that should catch them. Two further mutations were genuine equivalent mutants (identical x86 codegen), correctly not flagged. Nothing in `c_src/` was modified."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":211,"cache_read_input_tokens":159849,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":211,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7721d2ca-c5d4-46f5-9b61-8d143d83a75e","uuid":"40bbe28f-dafe-40b6-a302-03f121d7c3ad","timestamp":"2026-08-19T11:22:02.571Z"}
